# The terminal a Terminal=true desktop entry opens in, per the XDG terminal
# spec draft: one desktop-entry id per line, first one that exists wins.
#
# vicinae reads this FIRST in XdgAppDatabase::terminalEmulator(), ahead of
# niri-xdg-terminals.list, the xdg-terminal-exec binary, the
# x-scheme-handler/terminal default, and finally a hardcoded scan of entries
# carrying Categories=TerminalEmulator. That last step is the bug this file
# exists to kill: foot ships THREE such entries and one of them,
# foot-server.desktop, is Exec=foot --server — a daemon, not a window. On the
# mini vicinae picked it and ran `foot --server --app-id btop.desktop -e btop`,
# which races the already-running foot-server.socket and dies; btop never
# appeared and nothing was reported, since the launch is fire-and-forget
# (found 2026-08-26, btop from vicinae). The MacBook only worked by accident:
# a stray alacritty left over from the OSC 72 drag-and-drop test outranked
# foot-server in that same scan.
#
# footclient first for the same reason bin/foot-run reaches for it — the
# socket-activated server shares glyph caches and spawns instantly. vicinae
# knows this binary by name and drives it as `footclient --app-id <id> -e <cmd>`,
# so the window lands with the entry's id (btop.desktop), not `foot`; the
# niri scroll-factor rule keyed on ^foot$ therefore misses these. foot.desktop
# is the standalone fallback if the client entry ever goes missing.
footclient.desktop
foot.desktop
